PCI: tegra194: set the core clock for the link's speed

The core clock table has a dummy first entry so that it is indexed by the
link speed itself, but the one line that sets the rate still indexes it
with speed - 1. Every controller runs one generation slow: the SSD's
Gen3 x4 link runs its controller at 125 MHz instead of 250 MHz. Index the
table by the speed, as mainline and L4T R36 do.

At 125 MHz the controller still carries a Gen3 x4 link at full rate, with
almost no margin. On the PAB's SSD the fix changes neither throughput nor
queue-depth-1 latency.

Applies to the Linux 6.8 tree in L4T R39.2.1.
---
diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-tegra194.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-tegra194.c
index 4070105..03b6e69 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-tegra194.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-tegra194.c
@@ -473,7 +473,7 @@ static void tegra_pcie_icc_set(struct tegra_pcie_dw *pcie)
 		speed = 0;
 
 	if ((speed > 0) && (speed <= 4) && !pcie->is_safety_platform)
-		clk_set_rate(pcie->core_clk, pcie_gen_freq[speed - 1]);
+		clk_set_rate(pcie->core_clk, pcie_gen_freq[speed]);
 }
 
 static void apply_bad_link_workaround(struct dw_pcie_rp *pp)
